.TH QSEAL 1 "November 2026" "QSEAL 1.0.0" "Quantum-Secure Enterprise Access & Link Manual"
.SH NAME
qseal \- Quantum-Secure Enterprise Access & Link Platform
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qseal
[\fIcommand\fR] [\fIsubcommand\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B qseal-server
[\fIsubcommand\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B qseal-client
[\fIsubcommand\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBQSEAL\fR is an independent, high-performance secure networking platform operating over native UDP sockets.
It provides quantum-resistant mutual authentication, post-quantum key establishment, authenticated encryption, and sliding replay protection without relying on third-party coordination servers, Tailscale, or centralized VPN infrastructure.

QSEAL supports two cryptographic profiles:
.IP \[bu] 2
\fBHybrid Mode\fR: Combines classical X25519 ECDH key exchange with post-quantum ML-KEM-768 (NIST FIPS 203) using HKDF-SHA-384, authenticated via ML-DSA-65 (NIST FIPS 204) digital signatures and encrypted with ChaCha20-Poly1305 (RFC 8439).
.IP \[bu] 2
\fBPure PQC Mode\fR: Uses exclusively standardized post-quantum primitives (ML-KEM-768, ML-DSA-65) and ChaCha20-Poly1305.

.SH PLATFORM COMMANDS
.TP
.B setup
Launch the interactive onboarding setup wizard to benchmark hardware, configure networking, select cryptographic profiles, and generate identity keys.
.TP
.B detect
Analyze host architecture, SIMD CPU extensions (AVX2, NEON, AES-NI), memory budgets, and cryptographic provider readiness.
.TP
.B diagnostics
Execute end-to-end network reachability, UDP port availability, NAT/CGNAT classification, and cryptographic verification checks.
.TP
.B status
Display global platform status, active configuration paths, server daemon state, and client connectivity.
.TP
.B clients
Display authoritative real-time table of all authenticated active client sessions connected to the local server.
.TP
.B benchmark
Execute microbenchmarks for KEX key generation, encapsulation, decapsulation, DSA signing, DSA verification, and AEAD encryption.

.SH SERVER COMMANDS
.TP
.B server start [\fIport\fR]
Launch the independent QSEAL UDP server in the foreground. Default UDP port is 9877.
.TP
.B server stop
Gracefully terminate the background QSEAL server process using SIGTERM.
.TP
.B server restart
Restart the running QSEAL server.
.TP
.B server status
Query authoritative runtime telemetry, listening address, advertised endpoint, uptime, and active client counts.
.TP
.B server clients
Print detailed table of all connected clients, device IDs, remote IP:Port endpoints, session IDs, and traffic statistics.
.TP
.B server show-logs
Display recent log entries from the server log file.
.TP
.B server validate
Validate the syntax, network attributes, and permissions of the server configuration file.
.TP
.B server init
Initialize cryptographic identity keys and generate default YAML configuration.

.SH CLIENT COMMANDS
.TP
.B client connect [\fIhost\fR] [\fIport\fR]
Establish a quantum-safe encrypted UDP connection to the remote server.
.TP
.B client disconnect
Terminate the active client connection.
.TP
.B client status
Display client tunnel status and session telemetry.
.TP
.B client show-logs
Display recent client log output.
.TP
.B client validate
Validate the syntax and permissions of the client configuration file.
.TP
.B client init
Initialize client cryptographic identity keys and generate default YAML configuration.

.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " path"
Specify explicit path to configuration YAML or JSON file.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " port"
Specify UDP listening or target port (default: 9877).
.TP
.BI \-\-bind " ip"
Specify local IP address to bind server socket (default: 0.0.0.0).
.TP
.BI \-\-advertised " endpoint"
Specify public-facing WAN IP:Port or hostname advertised to external clients.
.TP
.BI \-\-server " host"
Remote server hostname or IPv4 address.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " hybrid|pqc"
Select cryptographic profile (default: hybrid).
.TP
.BI \-\-msg " text"
Transmit a single encrypted message and disconnect (non-interactive).
.TP
.B \-\-non-interactive
Execute automated initialization without interactive prompts.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print command usage summary and exit.

.SH CONFIGURATION FILES
.TP
.I /etc/qseal/server.yaml
Primary server configuration file.
.TP
.I /etc/qseal/client.yaml
Primary client configuration file.
.TP
.I /var/lib/qseal/
Directory containing identity private keys (permissions 0600) and public keys (permissions 0644).
.TP
.I /var/log/qseal/
Directory containing operational log files.
.TP
.I /run/qseal/qseal-server.status
Authoritative JSON runtime state maintained by the running server daemon.

.SH SYSTEMD SERVICES
.TP
.B qseal-server.service
Systemd unit managing the background server daemon.
.TP
.B qseal-client.service
Systemd unit managing the background client tunnel.

.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Private key files in \fI/etc/qseal/\fR and \fI/var/lib/qseal/\fR must strictly maintain file permissions of 0600.
Network deployments behind edge routers require UDP port 9877 to be forwarded to the server's private IP.
Servers behind Carrier-Grade NAT (CGNAT, RFC 6598) cannot accept unsolicited inbound connections; use a public static IP or cloud VPS.

